[Black "Introduction to Pattern Internalisation Essentials"]
[Result "*"]

 { One of the key advantages a human has over chess engines is the ability to recognise patterns. After sufficient exposure to the game, you probably would have noticed a certain type of checkmate or an opening trap occurring rather frequently. Some examples include a back rank checkmate or a trick on the f7/f2 square. These positions and tactical combinations may have then left a deep impression on you. As a result, whenever a similar position presents itself again, you are able to execute the correct series of moves instinctively. This is how pattern recognition works. The secret to rapid improvement lies in the ability to recognise a given pattern after only few exposures. Hence, to expedite your chess growth, I have developed a method which I will call Pattern Internalisation Essentials (PIE) . PIE is intended to help you effectively internalise new tactical patterns for which you have just found the correct sequence of moves. It comprises the following four steps: 1. Notice how the relevant chess pieces interact. Take note of the responsibilities of every piece in making the tactic possible. The more purposefully we pay attention to the dynamics between the pieces, the deeper we will appreciate the pattern. This enhances our ability to carry out the subsequent steps. 2. Determine the types of strategical weaknesses in your opponent’s position that pave the way for the tactic. This includes poor piece activity, weak squares, specific unprotected pieces, and many more. By training ourselves to identify the preconditions for a tactical combination, we are adding to our repertoire of tactical motifs. Ultimately, this will sharpen our tactical vision. For example, whenever we see our opponent having three pawns on their original squares in front of his King, we would instinctively find ways to manoeuvre a major piece towards the back rank. 3. Consider changing the position slightly in a realistic manner. You may change, add, or remove certain critical pieces. Check if the tactic still works, or if a different tactic becomes available. At first glance, this step may appear redundant. However, if you go through it diligently, you are poised to solve a wider range of similar positions from a single pattern. Take for example the back rank checkmate whose basic motif is three pawns in front of the King. Now, suppose one of the pawns had been shifted out of the second rank (e.g. h2-h3) , creating a flight square, h2, for the King. As long as this flight square is covered by one of our pieces (e.g. Bb8 or Ng4) , back rank checkmate remains a possibility. From this imaginative exercise arising from just one pattern (three pawns in front of King) , we are able to solve multiple positions may well present themselves from time to time in our games. In fact, following through this step yields a bigger benefit. You will develop a deeper appreciation of what you found from the earlier two steps. Let me explain. Suppose you have changed the position by giving the opponent an extra dark-squared Bishop. And then you realise that the particular tactic no longer works. This highlights the value of your pieces controlling dark squares (which you might have found in step 1) , and confirms that weak dark squares are a key strategical weakness in the opponent’s camp (which you might have found in step 2) . 4. Mentally play out the tactical combination a few times. Be sure to include critical variations when doing so. Making mental moves repeatedly helps sear the combination in your mind and acquaints you with the key moves. Unlike the methods presented in previous chapters, PIE can be approached a lot more flexibly. It is up to you how thoroughly and for how long you would like to execute each step. Using PIE on a single pattern can take anywhere between 20 seconds and 10 minutes. The whole idea is for you to have a deeper appreciation of the pattern. To progressively familiarise you with PIE, this chapter is divided into four sections. In the first, I will show you a set of 10 common checkmating patterns. Working with familiar patterns, it will be easier for you to understand how PIE can be used. I will share my own thought process in detail as I work through each step. Bear in mind that there are dozens of ways to carry out each step for any given pattern. So don’t be afraid to come up with your own ideas. In the second section, you will be given 5 relevant puzzles to put what you have learnt to practice. This is the “Aha!” moment for many students because they realise that PIE has enabled them to sense obscure mating possibilities in otherwise difficult positions. As a result, they are able to steer the game to desirable positions with ease. In the third section, we will then return to using PIE on another set of 10 patterns. But this time, they are significantly more complex and are taken from real games. This will give you a better idea of how to use PIE in practical situations. And in the final section, we will solve another 5 puzzles related to these 10 complex patterns. } 1. -- 
 *

