 { Now that you have applied PIE to the 10 mating patterns, you are armed with a new set of tactical motifs to work with. For example, when applying step 2 of the Burger Technique in this position, the White pawn on g6 should count as a tactical motif because it is a key feature of Damiano’s mate. Hence, when moving on to step 3 (calculating candidate moves), you should think of moves that allow you to land your Queen on h7 eventually. } ( { You may also recall the combination I showed with the Damiano’s mate pattern where White sacrificed with } 1. Rh8 { so that } Kxh8 { would allow the White Queen to land on the h-file with } 2. Qh1 { , followed by } Kg8 3. Qh7 { . However, our Queen doesn’t have access to the h-file right now. Therefore, using reciprocal thinking, we should think of ways to grant our Queen access to the h-file with tempo. And that’s how we arrive at … } ) 
1. { -KEY- } Bc7 $1 { Attacking Black's Queen while clearing the way for an eventual Qh6. } Qxc7 2. Rh8+ Kxh8 3. Qh6+ Kg8 4. Qh7+ Kf8 5. Qh8# { This is how you combine PIE with the Burger Technique - By using the key feature of a pattern as a tactical motif, and then selecting candidate moves through logical reasoning. Similar to how other motifs of the Burger Technique (e.g., unprotected pieces, trappable pieces, critical squares) can be identified intuitively with enough practice, these new motifs acquired through PIE will also eventually become part of your subconscious mind, allowing you to spot potential mates even in faster games. To see how effectively you have internalised the patterns and motifs, we will attempt some checkmating puzzles. Be sure to keep an eye out for the new motifs we have found from the previous patterns. I will not provide any hints. However, the explanations and ideal thought process to solve each puzzle will be revealed only after you have completed it. } 
