 { PIE can be used at any time you see an unfamiliar tactical pattern, which can be found just about anywhere – from puzzle books and game analyses to chess live streams and Youtube videos. However, there are times you may find patterns which are so novel that you doubt you’ll ever see it in your own games. It is all to easy to dismiss them as one-offs which aren’t worthy to be internalised. On the contrary, these combinations are gold mines that will give you the edge to outsmart your opponents. After all, if we only study common patterns, how will we ever see tactics that our opponents can’t? Furthermore, studying complex and obscure combinations will significantly improve our tactical senses. For example, let’s suppose we have found a tactical shot emerging from a seemingly serene position. PIE will reveal the characteristics of that position which were precursors to the tactical combination. This enables us to evaluate positions more accurately, knowing whether a position is truly devoid of tactics. With this in mind, we will practice applying PIE in tougher and more practical situations. Instead of giving familiar patterns and isolating them as seen in the earlier part of this chapter, we will be looking at 10 tactical shots taken from real games. Again, I will list the steps of PIE at the start of every position and offer my observations along the way. Feel free to add on additional observations which I may not have included. At the end, I will test your knowledge with 5 relevant puzzles. } 1. -- 
