16. { -KEY- } Nxd5 $4 { Svidler thought that he was winning a free pawn. Switching move orders does not help White win the pawn } ( 16. Bxf6 Qxf6 17. Nxd5 Qd6 { Black will win at least the h2 pawn back } ) 
Qxd5 17. Bxf6 Bb7 $1 { The key zwischenzug! Black is threatening mate on g2 while defending the Queen. White has to defend g2 in the next move. After which, Black will safely recapture on f6, remaining up a piece for just one pawn. Svidler immediately resigned here. Let's use PIE to understand what has just happened. } ( 17... Bxf6 $4 18. Bh7 Kxh7 19. Qxd5 { This is the line Svidler had considered. However, Malaniuk was not obliged to recapture the f6 Bishop on the 17th move. 1. Piece interaction and responsibilities: (i) Black’s d8 Queen keeps an eye on d5. (ii) Black’s f6 Knight temporarily defends the Queen just before White plays Bxf6. (iii) Black’s c8 Bishop is ready to spring to life at b7, defending the Queen on d5 and threatening mate just in the nick of time. (iv) Black’s e8 Rook overprotects the e5 Bishop, which is attacked by the White f6 Bishop after move 17. 2. Strategical weaknesses: (i) White left g2 insufficiently defended. (ii) White’s h1-a8 diagonal is weak. 3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail: (i) If Black did not have the Rook on e8 defending the e5 Bishop. White will get away with the extra pawn after 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 17. Bxf6 Bb7 18. Bh7+ Kxh7 19. Qxd5 Bxd5 20. Bxe5 (ii) If Black did not have a Bishop on e5. The reason the combination would not work is that White will have a way of saving g2 and his f6 Bishop simultaneously with 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 17. Bxf6 Bb7 18. Qg4! Hence, to add on to step 1, the e5 Bishop is important to attack the f6 Bishop in this line. (iii) If White had his Rook on e1. The e5 Bishop will no longer be overprotected and this becomes an issue after 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 17. Bxf6 Bb7 18. Bh7+ Kxh7 19. Qxd5 Bxd5 20. Bxe5 (iv) If White had a Knight one move away from e3, such as f1. White will have 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 17. Bxf6 Bb7 18. Ne3 to simultaneously defend g2 and gain a valuable tempo on the d5 Queen, buying the time needed to save the f6 Bishop. 3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work: (i) If White had a Knight one move away from f4. While a Knight can defend mate and attack the d5 Queen from f4, our e5 Bishop is watching that square. 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 17. Bxf6 Bb7 18. Nf4 Bxf4 } ) 

