 { This game was played in the Runavik Open back in 2017 and remains one of Sarin's fastest victories. His opponent resigned immediately after 13. Nxf7 but let's take a look at what could have happened } 13. { -KEY- } Nxf7 { It is worthwhile noting that aside from attacking the d8 Queen, 13. Nxf7 comes with an auxiliary threat of 14. Bxg6 hxg6 15. Qh8+ Kxf7 16. Ng5# } Kxf7 ( 13... Qc7 14. Bxg6 hxg6 15. Qh8 Kxf7 16. Ng5 ) 
14. Ng5+ Kg8 15. Nxe6 ( 15. Qxe6 Kg7 16. Qf7 Kh8 17. Ne6 { Let's now carry out PIE. 1. Piece interaction and responsibilities: (i) White e5 Knight attacks f7. (ii) White f3 Knight is prepared to attack f7 and e6 . (iii) White Queen attacks e6. (iv) Every Black piece around the Queen traps her. 2. Strategical weaknesses: (i) f7 is unprotected. (ii) e6 is unprotected. (iii) All of White’s minor pieces and the Queen are poised to attack the Kingside. (iv) Black is extremely cramped because he has less space and has too many pieces. 3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail: (i) If Black’s e8 Rook were on f8, protecting f7. (ii) If Black’s e7 Bishop were on f8, allowing the e8 Rook to defend e6. (iii) If White did not have his Knights on e5 and f3, or his Queen on h3. 3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work: (i) If White did not have a Bishop on d3. (ii) If White did not have a Bishop on f4. (iii) If Black’s c8 Rook were on a8. Even though this un-traps the Black Queen, we can play 15. Qxe6+ with an attack instead of 15. Nxe6. } ) 

