 { This combination by the great Alekhine started from the Caro-Kann, Two Knights Attack. } 11. { -KEY- } Nxf7 { Forking the Queen and the Rook. It may seem that Black's best option is to take the Knight, but that leads to a forced mate. 1. Piece interaction and responsibilities: (i) White’s e5 Knight attacks f7. (ii) White’s c4 Bishop and e2 Queen attack e6. (iii) Black’s h6 pawn and h8 Rook trap the King. That said, even without those two pieces, White’s attack will still lead to victory but it is not as swift. 2. Strategical weaknesses: (i) f7 was unprotected. (ii) e6 was very poorly defended. (iii) Black’s light squares are extremely weak with the Bishop marooned at h7. It could have saved the day if it was defending either e6 or f7. 3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail: (i) If Black’s h7 Bishop were on a square that defends e6 or f7, such as c8 or g8. (ii) If Black’s Queen were on c7, overprotecting f7 . (iii) If Black’s f8 Bishop were on e7, since after 11. Nxf7 Kxf7 12. Qxe6+ Ke8, 13. Qf7+ is not checkmate. 3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work: (i) If Black did not have a pawn on h6. This will provide an escape square for the King, but it is not a very good one. 11. Nxf7 Kxf7 12. Qxe6+ Kg7 13. Qf7+ Kh6 14. d3+ g5 15. hxg5# } Kxf7 12. Qxe6+ Kg7 { Forced. } 13. Qf7# 
