21. { -KEY- } Nd5 { Removing the f6 Knight from its defensive outpost } Nxd5 { Now that the Knight is no longer defending the King, Wei Yi brilliantly crashes through without even bothering to recapture the Knight. } ( 21... Bxd5 22. exd5 { White will continue with Qf3, g4 and g5, and the pressure on f7 is tremendous. If Black plays Nxd5, White will carry out a similar attack to what was seen in the game. } Nxd5 23. Rxf7 ) 
22. Rxf7 $3 { Luring the King out for a walk. This sacrifice must be accepted because of the mate threat, Qh7# } Kxf7 ( 22... Nf6 23. Qe6 Bf8 24. Re7 Kh8 25. Qxf6 Kg8 26. Qxg6 Kh8 27. Qh7 ) 
23. Qh7+ Ke6 24. exd5+ Kxd5 { Black is threatening to run with Kc6-c7-b8 } 25. Be4+ $1 { The only way to keep the attack going } Kxe4 26. Qf7 $1 { Taking away d5 } Bf6 27. Bd2+ Kd4 28. Be3+ Ke4 29. Qb3 $1 { Threatening Qd3# while keeping an eye on the d5 escape square } Kf5 30. Rf1+ Kg4 31. Qd3 Bxg2+ 32. Kxg2 Qa8+ 33. Kg1 Bg5 34. Qe2+ Kh4 35. Bf2+ Kh3 36. Be1 { With a decisive threat of 37. Rf3+ Kg4 38. Qg2+ Kh5 39. Rh3+ Bh4 40. Rxh4#, Bruzon resigned here. This is certainly a long line, but we can still apply PIE. To clarify, these observations are made on the position just before 21. Nd5 1. Piece interaction and responsibilities: (i) White’s c3 Knight is in a position to challenge the f6 Knight. (ii) White’s f1 Rook has an X-ray attack on f7. (iii) White’s h3 Queen is attacking h7. (iv) All of White’s remaining pieces are well-centralised, and are ready for the king hunt after the Black monarch gets drawn out. 2. Strategical weaknesses: (i) Black’s pieces are not defending the King well. Only the f6 Knight is a good defender. (ii) Black has too many open lines towards the King. 3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail: Given that all of White’s pieces are involved, changes in the position will likely alter the outcome of the attack. However, there are a few changes that will still allow the combination to work. These are listed in the next step. 3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work: (i) If the e3 Bishop were on d2 or c1. In fact, the attack will be even more direct since the e1 Rook protects e4. When Black eventually plays 24…Kd5, we can respond with the same good move, 25. Be4+, where 25…Kxe4 is not possible. (ii) If the d3 Bishop were on e2 instead. After 23…Ke6, we have 24. Bg4+ and mate to follow. } 
