45. { -KEY- } b6 { Shankland resigned here forgetting that this is a theoretical draw. To draw, Black's King just needs to shuffle between c8, d8 and d7 and White has no way of displacing it or attacking b7. If the Black King could reach a8, that would also be a draw for the same reason. } ( 45. b6 Kd6 46. Kg4 Kd7 47. Kxh3 Kc8 48. Bf4 Kd8 49. Kg4 Kd7 50. Kf5 Kc8 51. Ke6 Kd8 52. Kf7 Kc8 53. Ke7 { Stalemate. 1. Piece interaction and responsibilities: (i) Black’s b7 pawn blocks the enemy pawns and sits on a light square, immune to the White dark-squared Bishop. Additionally, it also contributes to the stalemate resource. (ii) Black’s King prevents the White King from accessing b7 from c7 or c8 (iii) White’s b6 pawn takes the c7 and a7 squares away from the Black King, contributing to the stalemate resource. Moreover, the b6 pawn prevents the White King from using the b6 square to attack b7. 2. Strategical weaknesses: (i) White has the Bishop on the wrong colour (or Black has the pawn on the right colour) . (ii) White’s b6 pawn occupied a square which the White King would want for itself. Moreover, that pawn also took away the a7 and c7 squares, helping Black stalemate itself. 3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular fortress fail: (i) If White’s b6 pawn were anywhere else. (ii) If White had a light-squared Bishop. White would simply sacrifice the Bishop for the b7 pawn and win the pawn endgame. 3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the fortress to work: (i) If Black had a couple of extra pawns on the Kingside. The excess reserve tempi do not change the fact that Black’s King cannot be displaced from guarding the b7 pawn. } ) 
Ng1 
