37... { -KEY- } Bxg7 38. Bxf5 Kxf5 39. Kxg7 { Many would mistakenly think this pawn endgame favours Black. However, Lasker knew this was, in fact, a draw and went on to trade Bishops confidently. } a5 40. h4 Kg4 41. Kg6 ( 41. Kf6 $4 { Starting the race on f6, the King is one move short of catching the Queenside pawns } c4 42. bxc4 bxc4 43. Ke5 c3 44. bxc3 a4 45. Kd4 a3 46. Kd3 a2 47. Kc2 a1=Q $11 ) 
Kxh4 42. Kf5 Kg3 { This time, the King started his race from g6, which made all the difference } ( 42... c4 43. bxc4 bxc4 44. Ke4 c3 45. bxc3 a4 46. Kd3 a3 47. Kc2 a2 48. Kb2 ) 
43. Ke4 Kf2 44. Kd5 Ke3 45. Kxc5 Kd3 46. Kxb5 Kc2 47. Kxa5 Kxb3 { 1. Piece interaction and responsibilities: (i) White’s h-pawn bought White an extra move, which was used to play 41. Kg6 (ii) White’s two Queenside pawns adequately held off Black’s majority. 2. Strategical weaknesses: Not applicable 3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail: (i) If Black had just one extra move. For example, if his a6 pawn were to start on a5 instead. (ii) If Black had an extra pawn on c7. At first glance, it might seem that a pawn on c7 does not contribute to the King-and-pawn race, but it does help Black to consolidate his Queenside more easily. For example, he could play 39... b4 40. h4 c4 41. bxc4 Kg4 42. Kg6 Kxh4 43. Kf5 a5 44. Ke4 a4 45. Kd3 c5 3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work: (i) If the Kings started a square to White’s left (i.e. After move 39, White’s King on f7, and Black’s King on e5) . Being closer to the Queenside and further from the Kingside can only benefit White. } 
