Kxh7 2. Rh1+ Kg7 3. Qh4 { With the h7 pawn gone, Black has neither Kh8 nor h5 to save him here } Rf7 { Trying to escape through f8 } 4. Qh8+ Kf8 5. Bxf7 { Weakening the 7th rank to allow the White pieces to penetrate } Qxf7 6. Rh7 Qe6 7. Qg7+ Ke8 8. Nd6+ Qxd6 ( 8... Kd8 9. Qc7 ) 
9. Qf7+ Kd8 10. Qxg8+ Qf8 11. Qxf8# { Now, let's carry out PIE. 1. Piece interaction and responsibilities: (i) One White Rook sacrificed itself for the h-pawn, paving the inroads for his brother. (ii) The other Rook was used to penetrate the newly opened h-file. (iii) The rest of White’s army was poised to attack the Black King. 2. Strategical weaknesses: (i) Black’s King hardly had any cover. (ii) Black’s already weak h-file was exacerbated by the g8 Knight, which prevented Black from defending with …Rh8 3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail: Interestingly, Black’s position was so bad that this combination will still work in one form or another despite certain minute changes to the position. I will show a few examples in step 3b. 3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work: If the White Knight were on g4 instead of e4, we would have 1. Rxh7+ Kxh7 2. Rh1+ Kg7 3. Qh4 Rf7 4. Nxe5 where our Knight cannot be taken. 4…Qxe5 5. Qh8+ Kf8 6. Qxe5 If the White Knight were on f4 instead of e4, we can play 1. Rxh7+ Kxh7 2. Rh1+ Kg7 3. Nh5+ Kh8 4. Nf6+ If the White Knight were on d4 instead of e4, we can still play 1. Rxh7+ Kxh7 2. Rh1+ Kg7 3. Bxg8 Kxg8 4. Nxf5 Rxf5 5. Qxg6+ Qg7 6. Qxf5 with a decisive attack It may seem that the Knight near the enemy King was key to the attack’s success. But what if Black had somehow managed to play …Bxe4 to get rid of the pesky Knight and White responded with fxe4 (The only changes from the initial position are that the e4 Knight and f5 Bishop are off the board and our f3 pawn is on e4) ? We would need to play a preparatory move. 1. Rdg1 threatening Rxh7+ followed by Qxg6. Black might try 1. …Qd6 to defend g6 but comes 2. Rxh7+! Kxh7 3. Rh1+ Kg7 4. Qh4 Ne7 5. Qh6+ Kf6 6. Rh3 with a decisive attack, yet again. } 
