50. { -KEY- } Qh6+ { This famous mate in 2 was played in the final match of the rapid tiebreaks in the 2016 World Championship between Magnus Carlsen and Sergei Karjakin. Whichever way Black takes the Queen, White would still have mate } gxh6 ( 50... Kxh6 51. Rh8 ) 
51. Rxf7# { 1. Piece interaction and responsibilities: (i) White’s f5 Rook covered g5 and attacked f7. (ii) White’s c8 Rook covered the back rank and was ready to move to h8. (iii) White’s h5 pawn took away g6. (iii) White’s Queen either diverted the Black King from defending h8, or the g7 pawn from defending the 7th rank. 2. Strategical weaknesses: (i) White was dangerously active, with three major pieces and a far-advanced pawn near the Black King. (ii) Black had little defense – the e7 Bishop and the f7 and g7 pawns were ineffective in keeping the King safe. 3a. Changes in the position that would make this particular combination fail: (i) If Black somehow had f7 protected, such as having a Bishop on b3. (ii) If Black’s e7 Bishop were on f6, blocking the attack on f7. 3b. Changes in the position that would still allow the tactic to work: (i) If White did not have the h5 pawn – White would have 50. Rh5+ Kg6 51. Qf5# (ii) If Black had an extra pawn on h6. } 
