 { This game was played at the Nutcracker Rapid 2017 between Vladislav Artimiev (White) and Shakhriyah Mamedyarov (Black) . } 13. { -KEY- } Nxd5 { Mamedyarov must have been familiar with the game between Peter Svidler and Vladimir P Malaniuk (Pattern #11) and thought that the d5 pawn was taboo. However, there is a key difference in the position - Black does not have a Bishop on e5! Recall that we used PIE and concluded that a Black needed a Bishop on e5 to recapture on f6. } Qxd5 14. Bxf6 Bb7 15. Qg4 $1 { Had the Black c5 Bishop been on e5, Black could have played Bxf6 here. Instead, Mamedyarov was forced to simplify into a worse endgame, from which Artemiev managed to squeeze a win. } Qxg2+ 16. Qxg2 Bxg2+ 17. Kxg2 { This rather recent example shows the importance of PIE (particularly the third step here) in ensuring that we avoid getting rooked by such deceptively similar tactical patterns. } 
 *

