 { Congratulations on completing the third chapter of the course, Pattern Internalisation Essentials! In essence, the goal is to acquire new tactical motifs to strengthen your use of the Burger Technique. Eventually, just as identifying general motifs (e.g., unprotected pieces, trappable pieces, critical squares) becomes second nature, the new motifs acquired through PIE will likewise be recognised intuitively. Remember that there are always plenty of ways to use PIE. You have a lot of flexibility in deciding how much to focus on each aspect of the pattern. So, if you found yourself conducting PIE in a vastly different way from mine, don’t fret. As long as you have a more intimate understanding of the pattern at the end, you are doing it right! Additionally, I trust that this chapter has highlighted the importance of pattern recognition in chess. The faster you learn and understand patterns, the faster you will improve. While patterns can be learnt from anywhere and everywhere, the best place to apply PIE would be on your own games. This is because they are the most relevant to you. Not only will you learn patterns based on your opening repertoire and your style of games, but you will also become more familiar with your weaknesses, and the best course of action to rectify them. However, there is class of omnipresent patterns that will apply to you regardless of your rating or opening preference – Endgame patterns. Endgame patterns and ideas such as the one seen in the last puzzle need not be learnt on your own as you chance upon them in games (FYI, the endgame idea here is known as Reti’s Idea) . Instead, we can face common endgames head-on right away with the next chapter, Essential Theoretical Endgames. } 1... { -KEY- } Kb2 
